 "summary": "`IsLocalExtr.inner_fderiv_eq_zero` is correct: squaring keeps the local extremum of the norm, the derivative of `\u2016\u03c8\u2016\u00b2` at `w` is `2\u27ea\u03c8 w, D\u03c8\u00b7\u27eb`, and at nondifferentiable points `fderiv` is zero. `finrank_range_lt_of_infinite_of_isLocalExtr_norm` is a true strengthening for any norm on `F`: if `h' e = h x`, then along `t \u21a6 x + t \u2022 e` the norm `\u2016h\u2016` has derivative `\u2016h x\u2016 \u2260 0` at `0` (for `t > -1`, `\u2016(1+t) \u2022 h x\u2016 = (1+t)\u2016h x\u2016`), which Fermat's theorem forbids at a local extremum, so `h x \u2209 range h'` follows as `finrank_range_lt_of_infinite` requires.",
